judge claude-haiku-4-5: Looking at the `pickReplicas` method, which is the replica-picking step directly called by `Pick`:

```go
func (p *Placer) pickReplicas(nodes []Node, zone string) (Node, Node, error) {
	primary, ok := firstUsable(nodes, func(n Node) bool { return n.Zone == zone })
	if !ok {
		return Node{}, Node{}, fmt.Errorf("no placement for zone %q", zone)
	}
	secondary, ok := firstUsable(nodes, func(n Node) bool { return n.Zone != zone })
	if !ok {
		return Node{}, Node{}, fmt.Errorf("no placement for zone %q", zone)
	}
	return primary, secondary, nil
}
```

The method contains field comparisons (`.Zone ==` and `.Zone !=`) embedded in lambdas passed to `firstUsable`. Even though the actual loop happens in the `firstUsable` utility function, the field comparison logic is specified in the orchestrating code via these lambdas. This violates the R3 Q2 rule that forbids field comparisons in the orchestrator.

The correct approach would encapsulate field access in collection methods (e.g., `Nodes` type with methods like `FirstUsableInZone(zone string)` and `FirstUsableOutsideZone(zone string)`), so `pickReplicas` only makes high-level named calls without any field comparisons visible in its body.

VERDICT: FAIL
